Here's a complete example in LATEX showcasing automatic table of contents generation using the built-in `\tableofcontents` command with the `titlesec` package for customization:

1. Create a new LATEX file named `mydocument.tex` with the following code snippet:

```tex
\documentclass[12pt, headings= /******/custom/, numberedbib]{book}   

\usepackage{titlesec, touppercase}   # for enhanced TOC customization

\addtocontents{toc}{\hskip\ågap} % define a gap between entries (customization)
\newcommand{\ågap}{\hspace{0em plus 2em}} 

\pagestyle{empty}  # No page numbers initially

\begin{document 

\titleformat*{\section}{\large\filcenter\bfseries} % format section style
\titleformat*{\subsection}{\large\filleft\bfseries} 

\preto\section{\addcontentsline{lot}{section}{#1}} % ensure automatic subsection listing

\thispagestyle{empty} % suppress first blank page

\setcounter{tocdepth}{3}  # Include up to subsection in ToC

\tableofcontents  # Generate automatic Table of Contents

\cleardoublepage  # Start content pages

... (Your document content begins here) 

%-------------------- Content with structured sections --------------------%

\section{Introduction}
\subsection{Overview}
\subsectionmethods{Methods and Algorithms}
\section{Results and Analysis}
\subsectionfindings{Findings}
\subsubsectionresults{Detailed Results}
\section{Discussion}
\subsectionimplications{Implications and Future Directions}

\... Additional chapters or sections with appropriate headings...

\listof{List of Abbreviations}{loa} % optional: add list with \newlist command

\printindex  # Generate index (if needed)

\end{document}
```

Explanation:

- We incorporate essential features for generating an automatic and customized table of contents (TOC) in this example. Don't forget to adjust the document structure and content according to your specific needs.

  - Load the `titlesec` package and `touppercase` for enhanced TOC formatting.
  - Customize the gap between TOC entries using `\ågap`.
  - `\preto` command ensures subsections are included in the TOC list (subsections appear under their parent sections).
  - Set the desired `\section`, `\subsection`, and `tocdepth` options for multi-level structure listing.
  - The `\tableofcontents` command generates the TOC automatically at the designated location.
  - Other relevant packages and customizations can be added based on your requirements.

After executing `latex`, `biber/bidix` (if using bibliography), and `pdfsequencing` tools (e.g., `latex`, `biber`, `latex twice`, and `pdfmerge`), you'll obtain the desired TOC within your compiled `mydocument.pdf`.

